<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arabia Petraea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">106 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> · Also known as Provincia Arabia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> Petraea was a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> formed in 106 CE when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> annexed the Nabataean kingdom. It covered southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, Jordan, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/negev">Negev</a> and the Sinai, with its capital at Bostra. A new road, the Via Nova Traiana, linked Bostra with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> at Aila. In the 4th century it was divided into smaller provinces.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arabia-petraea.jpg" alt="Arabia Petraea" width="320" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Empire_-_Arabia_Petraea_(125_AD).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Before 106 the area was ruled by the Nabataean Kingdom, whose last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabbel-ii">Rabbel II</a>, died in early 105. A succession crisis followed, and Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> refused to recognise any claimant. Instead he sent two legions: the Third Cyrenaica from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> advanced on Bostra from the south, and the Sixth Ferrata from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petra">Petra</a> from the north. There may have been little resistance, since the former royal troops of Nabataea joined the Roman forces. The new province, Arabia Petraea, meaning Rocky Arabia, was the only province in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> did not both gain and later lose during Trajan's reign. Petra was given the status of metropolis Arabica.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the annexation <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> built the Via Nova Traiana, about 430 km long, from the Syrian boundary to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>. It linked Bostra, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petra">Petra</a> and the route to Aila, now Aqaba, and eased administration, troop movement and taxation.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimius-severus">Septimius Severus</a> the province was enlarged northward at the expense of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, with the region of Shahba, birthplace of the later emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-arab">Philip the Arab</a>. Around 300, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>'s reforms, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/negev">Negev</a>, Sinai and southern Transjordan passed to Palaestina. In the second half of the 4th century the southern part of Palaestina, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petra">Petra</a> as capital, became Palaestina Tertia Salutaris, which replaced the name Arabia Petraea. The territory was briefly lost to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> in the war of 602 to 628 and then permanently to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Most of the province was desert, sparsely inhabited by nomadic Arab tribes, and its towns lay mainly in the north towards the Jordan and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hauran">Hauran</a>. The main cities were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petra">Petra</a>, Bosra, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerash">Gerasa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plovdiv">Philippopolis</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amman">Amman</a>, and the only major seaport was Ayla. Petra declined under Roman rule, partly because trade shifted to sea routes, and an earthquake in 363 damaged its water system, but papyri of the 530s to 590s found in a church show that it still flourished in the 6th century.</p><p>Greek civic life spread quickly. A new dating system, the Bostran era, began in 106, and the Babatha archive shows documents turning from Nabataean and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> to Greek, so that a deed of 110 is in Greek. Bostra, built in local basalt, looks severe and unclassical, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plovdiv">Philippopolis</a> was laid out on a Roman colonial plan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arabia Petraea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When King Rabbel II died, Trajan annexed it in 106 CE as the province of <b>Arabia Petraea</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/nabataean-kingdom">Nabataean Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
